local truncation error for the step from $x$ to $x_{new} = x + Δx$." 13194139535321 false] [17592186167136 1145 "The local truncation error (LTE) is the error made in a *single step* of a numerical method, when the step begins from an *exact value*. The LTE of a numerical method at step $n$ from $x$ with exact value ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)$ to ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{n} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-({y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)+Δy).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that the exact solution of the initial value problem is $y(x)={x}^{3}.$ Now, let's find the $y$-increment given by the numerical method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =({x}^{2}+{y}_{\\text{exact}}(x))\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+{x}^{3})\\cdot Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the exact value at ${x}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}}) & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(x+Δx) \\\\  & =(x+Δx)^{3} \\\\  & ={x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}\\cdot Δx+3x\\cdot (Δx)^{2}+(Δx)^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the LTE for step $n$ starting at $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{n} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\  & =[{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}\\cdot Δx+3x\\cdot (Δx)^{2}+(Δx)^{3}]-[{x}^{3}+({x}^{2}+{x}^{3})\\cdot Δx] \\\\  & ={x}^{3}-{x}^{3}+(3{x}^{2}-({x}^{2}+{x}^{3}))\\cdot Δx+3x\\cdot (Δx)^{2}+(Δx)^{3} \\\\  & =(2{x}^{2}-{x}^{3})Δx+3x(Δx)^{2}+(Δx)^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535321 true] [17592186167172 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535321 true] [17592186167172 1100 "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nAn initial value problem has the exact solution $y_{exact}(x) = e^{x}$. A numerical method is used to approximate the solution to the initial value problem, resulting in the table below, where $y$ denotes the approximate solution at each step, and $τ_{n}$ is the local truncation error at each step. At each step, the exact value is used to approximate the new value.\n\nCalculate the local truncation error at each step. Round your answers to three decimal places.\n\n| $n$ | $x$ | $y$ | $τ_{n}$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1.000$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $0.25$ | $1.283$ | {{field-1}} |\n| $2$ | $0.5$ | $1.644$ | {{field-2}} |\n| $3$ | $0.75$ | $2.104$ | {{field-3}} |" 13194139535321 false] [17592186167172 1100 "An initial value problem has the exact solution ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)={e}^{x}.$ A numerical method is used to approximate the solution to the initial value problem, resulting in the table below, where $y$ denotes the approximate solution at each step, and ${τ}_{n}$ is the local truncation error at each step. At each step, the exact value is used to approximate the new value. \n\nCalculate the local truncation error at each step. Round your answers to three decimal places.\n\n| $n$ | $x$ | $y$ | ${τ}_{n}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1.000$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $0.25$ | $1.283$ | {{field-1}} |\n| $2$ | $0.5$ | $1.644$ | {{field-2}} |\n| $3$ | $0.75$ | $2.104$ | {{field-3}} |" 13194139535321 true] [17592186167172 1145 "The local truncation error (LTE) is the error made in a *single step* of a numerical method, when the step begins from an *exact value*. The LTE of a numerical method at step $n$ from $x$ with exact value ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)$ to ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{n} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-({y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)+Δy).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe find the LTE at each step, rounding to three decimal places in each case:\n\n- After the first step, we have ${y}_{\\text{new}}=1.283.$ So, the LTE of the first step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{1} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(0.25)-1.283 \\\\  & ={e}^{0.25}-1.283 \\\\  & ≈0.001.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- After the second step, we have ${y}_{\\text{new}}=1.644.$ So, the LTE of the second step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{2} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(0.5)-1.644 \\\\  & ={e}^{0.5}-1.644 \\\\  & ≈0.005.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- After the third step, we have ${y}_{\\text{new}}=2.104.$ So, the LTE of the third step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{3} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(0.75)-2.104 \\\\  & ={e}^{0.75}-2.104 \\\\  & ≈0.013.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed table is as given below.\n\n| $n$ | $x$ | $y$ | ${τ}_{n}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1.000$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $0.25$ | $1.283$ | $0.001$ |\n| $2$ | $0.5$ | $1.644$ | $0.005$ |\n| $3$ | $0.75$ | $2.104$ | $0.013$ |" 13194139535321 true] [17592186167182 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535321 true] [17592186167182 1100 "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nA numerical method is used to approximate a solution to an initial value problem, resulting in the table below. If the exact solution is $y(x) = \\cos x$, find, rounded to three decimal places, the global truncation error at each step.\n\n| $n$ | $x_{n}$ | $y_{n}$ | $e_{n}$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $0.3$ | $0.943$ | {{field-1}} |\n| $2$ | $0.6$ | $0.793$ | {{field-2}} |\n| $3$ | $0.9$ | $0.595$ | {{field-3}} |" 13194139535321 false] [17592186167182 1100 "A numerical method is used to approximate a solution to an initial value problem, resulting in the table below. If the exact solution is $y(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ find, rounded to three decimal places, the global truncation error at each step.\n\n| $n$ | ${x}_{n}$ | ${y}_{n}$ | ${e}_{n}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $0.3$ | $0.943$ | {{field-1}} |\n| $2$ | $0.6$ | $0.793$ | {{field-2}} |\n| $3$ | $0.9$ | $0.595$ | {{field-3}} |" 13194139535321 true] [17592186167182 1145 "The global truncation error (GTE) is the total error accumulated after several steps of a numerical method. The GTE of a numerical method at step $n$ from ${x}_{n-1}$ to ${x}_{n}={x}_{n-1}+Δx$ is\n\n$$\n{e}_{n}=y({x}_{n})-{y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $y({x}_{n})$ is the exact value at ${x}_{n},$ and ${y}_{n}$ is the numerical value produced after $n$ steps.\n\nWe are given the numerical values ${y}_{n}$ produced at each step in the table. Now, we compute the exact value $y({x}_{n})$ at each value of ${x}_{n}:$\n\n- The exact value at ${x}_{0}=0$ is $y(0)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡0=1.$\n\n- The exact value at ${x}_{1}=0.3$ is $y(0.3)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0.3).$\n\n- The exact value at ${x}_{2}=0.6$ is $y(0.6)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0.6).$\n\n- The exact value at ${x}_{3}=0.9$ is $y(0.9)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0.9).$\n\nLet's add these values to the table.\n\n| $n$ | ${x}_{n}$ | ${y}_{n}$ | $y({x}_{n})$ | ${e}_{n}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $0.3$ | $0.943$ | $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0.3)$ |  |\n| $2$ | $0.6$ | $0.793$ | $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0.6)$ |  |\n| $3$ | $0.9$ | $0.595$ | $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0.9)$ |  |\n\nNow we compute the global truncation error at each step, rounding to three decimal places:\n\n- The GTE of the first step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}_{1} & =y({x}_{1})-{y}_{1} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0.3)-0.943 \\\\  & ≈0.012.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The GTE of the second step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}_{2} & =y({x}_{2})-{y}_{2} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0.6)-0.793 \\\\  & ≈0.032.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The GTE of the third step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}_{3} & =y({x}_{3})-{y}_{3} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0.9)-0.595 \\\\  & ≈0.027.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed table is as given below.\n\n| $n$ | ${x}_{n}$ | ${y}_{n}$ | $y({x}_{n})$ | ${e}_{n}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $0.3$ | $0.943$ | $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0.3)$ | $0.012$ |\n| $2$ | $0.6$ | $0.793$ | $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0.6)$ | $0.032$ |\n| $3$ | $0.9$ | $0.595$ | $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0.9)$ | $0.027$ |" 13194139535321 true] [17592186343613 1034 "0.002" 13194139535321 true] [17592186343613 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535321 true] [17592186343613 1111 #uuid "8ba70fe6-872d-5430-adff-3dce55ddc96f" 13194139535321 true] [17592186343614 1034 "0.001" 13194139535321 true] [17592186343614 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535321 true] [17592186343614 1111 #uuid "e92ec6fc-5fa1-550a-bd4b-0cce9dfe1a76" 13194139535321 true] [17592186343615 1034 "0.005" 13194139535321 true] [17592186343615 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535321 true] [17592186343615 1111 #uuid "82931969-beeb-522e-9e04-be42c527b26d" 13194139535321 true] [17592186343616 1034 "0.013" 13194139535321 true] [17592186343616 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535321 true] [17592186343616 1111 #uuid "2caa1575-0417-5abb-9f4e-c6471e7597fe" 13194139535321 true] [17592186343617 1017 #edb/ref 17592186343618 13194139535321 true] [17592186343617 1089 "q-333236" 13194139535321 true] [17592186343617 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535321 true] [17592186343617 1100 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x+2,\\,y(0)=0\n$$\n\nSuppose we wish to approximate the solution using the following numerical method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =(x+{y}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the exact solution is ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+2x,$ compute the local truncation error, ${τ}_{1},$ for the first step starting at $x=0$ with step size $Δx=0.3.$\n\n${τ}_{1}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535321 true] [17592186343617 1113 #uuid "e9a4071c-d689-56c8-a3f6-d39803db33e6" 13194139535321 true] [17592186343617 1145 "The local truncation error (LTE) is the error made in a *single step* of a numerical method, when the step begins from an *exact value*. The LTE of a numerical method at step $n$ from $x$ with exact value ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)$ to ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{n} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-({y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)+Δy).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that the exact $y$-value at the start of the step $x=0$ is $y(0)=0.$ Now, let's find the \n$y$-increment given by the numerical method.\n\nFirst, the slope at the start of the step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{exact}}^{′}(0) & =0+2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the increment at this step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =(x+{y}_{\\text{exact}}^{′}(0))\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(0+2)\\cdot 0.3 \\\\  & =0.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the exact value at ${x}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}}) & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(x+Δx) \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(0+0.3) \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(0.3) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(0.3)^{2}+2(0.3) \\\\  & =0.045+0.6 \\\\  & =0.645.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the LTE for the first step starting at $x=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{1} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-(y(0)+Δy) \\\\  & =0.645-(0+0.6) \\\\  & =0.045.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535321 true] [17592186343618 1000 #edb/ref 17592186343619 13194139535321 true] [17592186343618 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535321 true] [17592186343618 1041 "field-1" 13194139535321 true] [17592186343618 1104 #edb/ref 17592186343619 13194139535321 true] [17592186343618 1110 #uuid "7ee50bf1-2695-5c4e-af02-4baa131d1fcf" 13194139535321 true] [17592186343619 1034 "0.045" 13194139535321 true] [17592186343619 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535321 true] [17592186343619 1111 #uuid "672bae46-4731-52dc-b2f5-4b65df43e130" 13194139535321 true] [17592186343620 1017 #edb/ref 17592186343621 13194139535321 true] [17592186343620 1089 "q-333497" 13194139535321 true] [17592186343620 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535321 true] [17592186343620 1100 "Suppose we wish to approximate the solution to an initial value problem using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf the exact solution is ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)=3{x}^{2}+5x,$ find an expression for the local truncation error for the step from $x$ to ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx.$" 13194139535321 true] [17592186343620 1113 #uuid "8128dafc-bb62-5079-b47a-aed1e51ccf57" 13194139535321 true] [17592186343620 1145 "The local truncation error (LTE) is the error made in a single step of a numerical method, when the step begins from an exact value. The LTE of a numerical method at step $n$ from $x$ with exact value ${y}_{\\text{exact}}$ to ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{n} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-({y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)+Δy).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that the exact solution of the initial value problem is ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)=3{x}^{2}+5x.$ Now, let's find the $y$-increment given by the numerical method.\n\nFirst, the slope at a step starting at $x$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{exact}}^{′}(x)=(3{x}^{2}+5x)^{′}=6x+5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the increment given by the numerical method is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}^{′}(x)\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(6x+5)\\cdot Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the exact value at ${x}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}}) & =({y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)+Δx) \\\\  & =3(x+Δx)^{2}+5(x+Δx) \\\\  & =3({x}^{2}+2x\\cdot Δx+(Δx)^{2})+5x+5\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}+6x\\cdot Δx+3\\cdot (Δx)^{2}+5x+5\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}+(6x+5)\\cdot Δx+3\\cdot (Δx)^{2}+5x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the LTE for step $n$ starting at $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{n} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-({y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)+Δy) \\\\  & =[3{x}^{2}+(6x+5)\\cdot Δx+3\\cdot (Δx)^{2}+5x]-[3{x}^{2}+5x+(6x+5)\\cdot Δx] \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}-3{x}^{2}+((6x+5)-(6x+5))\\cdot Δx+5x-5x+3\\cdot (Δx)^{2} \\\\  & =3\\cdot (Δx)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535321 true] [17592186343621 1000 #edb/ref 17592186343622 13194139535321 true] [175921863